/// Who settled it and when, written by the system at a transition.
///
/// ─── Four spellings in two prototypes, eight chains in one HRMS ───────────
///
/// connectteam `LeaveRequest.decidedAt/decidedById` and
/// `Timesheet.approvedAt/approvedById`; jazzhr `Offer.respondedAt` and
/// `Scorecard.submittedAt`; outside the repo documenso's signing status,
/// mastodon's four moderation decisions, hrms with eight approval chains.
/// Every one is the same two facts — an instant and an actor — spelled for
/// the verb that happened to be on the transition. Half of them keep the
/// actor; half keep only the instant and then cannot answer "who approved
/// this", which is the first question an approval ever gets asked.
///
/// The transition itself is already declared:
///
///     @@transitions(status,
///       approve: requested -> approved @gate(5),
///       refuse:  requested -> refused  @gate(5))
///
/// The stamp is the half nobody declares, and this trait is that half under
/// one name. A hook on the transition writes both columns; `@system` keeps a
/// caller from writing them, because a decision a requester could backdate
/// is not a decision.
///
/// ─── Why one stamp and not one per verb ───────────────────────────────────
///
/// `Timesheet` has approve, reopen and export. A column pair per verb is six
/// columns and a row that remembers only the last of each; a `Decision` pair
/// records the latest settling and the audit trail (`@@trail(audit)`) records
/// the rest with their actors for free. A host that genuinely needs two
/// standing stamps — submitted AND approved, both shown — declares the second
/// pair under its own names; `@seals` on the approving transition is what
/// makes the sealed one permanent.
///
/// ─── What this wants to become ────────────────────────────────────────────
///
/// A stamp belongs on the transition that produces it, something like
/// `approve: requested -> approved @stamps(decidedAt, decidedById)`, so the
/// schema says which edge writes it rather than a hook. That is a language
/// question for litestone and not a catalog one; until it is answered this
/// trait is the spelling, and a host that spreads it has nothing to rename
/// when the attribute arrives.
///
/// ─── What is deliberately NOT here ────────────────────────────────────────
///
/// **No `decision` enum.** The verdict is the status column the transition
/// already moved. A second column saying `approved` beside a status saying
/// `approved` is two answers.
///
/// **No `reason`.** A refusal's reason is a product column (`note`,
/// `cancellationReason`) with its own length and its own visibility; three
/// instances have one and no two agree on who may read it.
trait Decision {
  /// The instant the transition ran. Nullable until it has.
  decidedAt   DateTime? @system

  /// The identity model's key, a column and not a relation because this file
  /// declares no `User`; the host may add the relation, and most do not,
  /// because the actor may since have left and the stamp must outlive them.
  decidedById String?   @system

  /// Every pending-queue read is "the undecided ones, oldest first".
  @@index([decidedAt])
}
